      "wmethod.bridge.logic.title": "Open the Logic Coverage Explorer \u2014 distinguishing sequences play the role that clause-distinguishing rows play for predicates.",
      "wmethod.quiz.prompt": "Under the W-method, how does the test-suite size grow with the number of states n (for a fixed alphabet and m)?",
      "wmethod.quiz.a": "It does not grow with n \u2014 the characterizing set has constant size.",
      "wmethod.quiz.b": "Exponentially in n, since every pair of states needs its own dedicated test.",
      "wmethod.quiz.c": "Polynomially \u2014 roughly with n\xB2 (state cover \xD7 characterizing set), times |X|^(m+1).",
      "wmethod.quiz.d": "Linearly in n \u2014 exactly one test per state.",
      "wmethod.quiz.correct": "Correct. |P| and |W| each scale with n, and the X^\u2264m middle adds a |X|^(m+1) factor \u2014 so the suite grows polynomially, not exponentially.",
      "wmethod.quiz.wrong": "Not quite. |P| \u2248 n and |W| \u2248 n, and the X^\u2264m segment contributes |X|^(m+1); the product is polynomial in n, not constant, linear, or exponential.",
      "wmethod.lab.prompt": "The W-method guarantees fault detection only if the implementation has at most m extra states beyond the spec. Is that assumption realistic for real systems? What happens to the guarantee when it is violated \u2014 and how would you choose m in practice?",
      // EFSM / Guarded-Transition Explorer (L4)
      "mbtTab.efsm": "EFSM Guards",
      "efsm.title": "EFSM / Guarded-Transition Explorer",
