      "gate.contrast": "DoR criteria are about clarity \u2014 can we start? DoD criteria are about completeness \u2014 are we finished? A weak gate does not stop the work; it just lets the defect through to a more expensive stage.",
      "gate.bridge.defectcost.title": "Open the Defect Cost Explorer \u2014 quantifies how much more a leaked defect costs downstream.",
      "gate.bridge.bdd.title": "Open the BDD / Gherkin Explorer \u2014 testable acceptance criteria are exactly what a DoR criterion demands.",
      "gate.quiz.prompt": "What is the essential difference between the Definition of Ready and the Definition of Done?",
      "gate.quiz.a": "They are the same checklist, just applied twice for safety.",
      "gate.quiz.b": "DoR is written by the team; DoD is written by the customer.",
      "gate.quiz.c": "DoR gates a story into the sprint \u2014 is it clear enough to start? DoD gates it out \u2014 is it truly finished?",
      "gate.quiz.d": "DoD is optional; only the Definition of Ready is mandatory in Scrum.",
      "gate.quiz.correct": "Correct. DoR is an entry gate (clarity to start), DoD is an exit gate (completeness to finish) \u2014 different questions at different points.",
      "gate.quiz.wrong": "Not quite. DoR is the entry gate \u2014 is the story clear enough to start? \u2014 and DoD is the exit gate \u2014 is it truly finished? Two different gates, two different questions.",
      "gate.lab.prompt": 'Which Definition of Done criterion does your team quietly treat as "optional"? Name it, and describe a defect that has shipped \u2014 or could ship \u2014 because that criterion is skipped.',
      // Three Amigos / Example Mapping Explorer (M5)
      "agileTab.examplemap": "Example Mapping",
      "emap.title": "Three Amigos / Example Mapping Explorer",
      "emap.desc": "Quality starts at story refinement, before code. The Three Amigos \u2014 business analyst, developer, tester \u2014 run Example Mapping: a user story (yellow) breaks into rules (blue), each illustrated by concrete examples (green), with open questions (red). Too many red cards means the story is not ready.",
      "emap.role.ba": "Business analyst",
      "emap.role.ba.note": "The BA brings the intent \u2014 why the story matters and what business rules it must honour.",
      "emap.role.dev": "Developer",
      "emap.role.dev.note": 'The developer brings feasibility \u2014 edge cases, technical constraints, and "what happens if\u2026" questions.',
      "emap.role.tester": "Tester",
      "emap.role.tester.note": "The tester brings doubt \u2014 the missing examples, the ambiguous wording, the cases nobody thought to ask about.",
      "emap.kind.story": "Story",
      "emap.kind.rule": "Rule",
